## docs assertions
72:- **Root-path target**: Accepts a `TerminalTarget` with a workspace root path (and optional display metadata) so desktop wrappers can connect without materializing workspace/file-explorer state.
150:### Mobile Phone Access
152:Phase One Android pairing removes the mobile Tools/Terminal/VNC page entirely. The interactive terminal remains a desktop/workspace tool, not a standard `/mobile` Phone Access surface. Historical terminal-command tool output may still appear as read-only agent activity in the mobile Activity digest.
156:The terminal session connects to `/ws/terminal/{sessionId}?cwd={encodedRootPath}` and communicates via WebSocket using JSON messages:
185:| `acquireFileExplorerLiveSession(workspaceId, consumerId)` | Registers one visible file explorer consumer. The first consumer opens one WebSocket/live watcher stream for the workspace and returns an idempotent release function. |
186:| `releaseFileExplorerLiveSession(workspaceId, consumerId)` | Releases a visible consumer. The last release disconnects the WebSocket and lets the backend stop the watcher lease. |
470:The frontend sends a `SearchFiles` GraphQL query. Search is a request/response snapshot operation and must not start a live watcher. See [autobyteus-server-ts/docs/modules/file_explorer.md](../../autobyteus-server-ts/docs/modules/file_explorer.md) for backend implementation details including:
526:Real-time file synchronization is **visibility-driven**. Snapshot APIs such as workspace create/fetch, folder lazy-load, file content reads, file mutations, and search are request/response flows and do **not** keep a backend filesystem watcher open. A live watcher exists only while at least one visible `FileExplorer.vue` consumer has acquired a live session for a workspace.
539:    ? workspaceStore.acquireFileExplorerLiveSession(workspaceId, liveSessionConsumerId)
551:acquireFileExplorerLiveSession(workspaceId: string, consumerId: string): () => void {
565:  return () => this.releaseFileExplorerLiveSession(workspaceId, consumerId);
609:The backend WebSocket route resolves the workspace and asks its file explorer for a watcher lease before subscribing to events. `WorkspaceFileExplorer.acquireWatcherLease("file-explorer-websocket")` starts the underlying `FileSystemWatcher` only for the first active lease and returns an idempotent lease release handle. `subscribe()` is valid only after a lease has started the watcher.
611:Each WebSocket session owns one watcher lease through `FileExplorerSession`. Disconnecting the WebSocket, ending the async event iterator, early-closing before the `CONNECTED` message is observed, or closing the workspace releases the session and lease. When the lease count reaches zero, the backend stops the chokidar watcher.
615:- `createWorkspace`, `fetchAllWorkspaces`, `folderChildren`, `fileContent`, and `searchFiles` return snapshots without acquiring watcher leases.
632:    ExplorerA->>Store: acquireFileExplorerLiveSession(workspaceId, consumerA)
640:    ExplorerB->>Store: acquireFileExplorerLiveSession(workspaceId, consumerB)
654:    Backend->>Explorer: release watcher lease
672:- `autobyteus-server-ts/src/services/file-explorer-streaming/file-explorer-stream-handler.ts` - watcher lease acquisition, session setup, stream loop, disconnect cleanup.
673:- `autobyteus-server-ts/src/services/file-explorer-streaming/file-explorer-session.ts` - session-owned async iterator cancellation and watcher lease release.
674:- `autobyteus-server-ts/src/file-explorer/file-explorer.ts` - lazy tree/search/operation/watcher capability boundary and watcher lease counting.
